{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011Cfb7hrvApicotJzcQms1A","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe model/mapper half of this change is clean and idiomatic — the static `Owner.normalizeEmail`, the `@Named(\"normalizeEmail\")` mapper qualifier, and the JavaDoc all mirror the existing `normalizeTelephone` pattern exactly, which is the right shape. The schema/data/openapi additions are coherent and additive. The structural problem is confined to the controller.\n\nFindings:\n\n1. **Normalization responsibility is split across two sites for email but one for telephone.** For telephone, the single call site is the mapper qualifier (`@Mapping(target = \"telephone\", qualifiedByName = \"normalizeTelephone\")`); the entity's static method has exactly one caller. Email now has *two* callers — the mapper qualifier (create path) **and** an inline `Owner.normalizeEmail(...)` in `OwnerRestControllerV1.updateOwner`. A maintainer tracing \"where does email get normalized?\" now has to find both. This is the kind of asymmetry that invites drift.\n\n2. **The update method treats two analogous fields inconsistently in adjacent lines.** Right next to each other it does:\n   ```java\n   currentOwner.setTelephone(ownerFieldsDto.getTelephone());            // raw\n   currentOwner.setEmail(Owner.normalizeEmail(ownerFieldsDto.getEmail())); // normalized\n   ```\n   Within one method, telephone is copied verbatim while email is normalized inline. Whatever the intended convention is, these two fields should follow it identically; as written it reads like an accident and future owner fields won't have a clear pattern to copy.\n\n3. **Normalization logic is leaking into the controller's altitude.** Every other field in `updateOwner` is a plain DTO→entity copy; the controller otherwise delegates transformation to the mapper. Introducing one field's business rule inline here makes the controller the only place that knows email must be lower-cased, which isn't where a reader of this codebase would look for it.\n\nSuggested direction (pick one and apply it to email so the split goes away): centralize the normalization so it holds on every persistence path regardless of whether the mapper is used — e.g. normalize inside `Owner.setEmail(...)` itself, or via a JPA `@PrePersist`/`@PreUpdate` lifecycle callback. Either makes the mapper qualifier and the controller call unnecessary, keeps the controller a plain copy, and guarantees create and update stay consistent. If you prefer to stay with the mapper-qualifier convention to match telephone, then route the update path through the mapper rather than hand-copying fields, so email isn't special-cased in the controller."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12076,"cache_read_input_tokens":11894,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12076},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c4ac5631-d039-4f97-8602-4243d7862a90","uuid":"acbb441e-e45d-4973-8fd6-bfcea3c5542a","timestamp":"2026-10-01T07:24:34.006Z","request_id":"req_011Cfb7hrYrTWk4w5xyAfuZR"}
